@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, tool list, thinking level, model, model endpoint,
  timeout,
  project root, general agent, fallback agent, child host options,
}
@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import { guided setup }
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  child host, parent model,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  task record, event log, parent session id, sessions folder, driver files,
}

component TaskCore {
  goal {
    Run each *task* from an agent definition and a brief on one child, keep it
    in a durable task record, let its caller send it more messages while it
    runs and after it settles, and return one *result* for each turn.
  }

  state {
    A task holds a *task id*, the frozen agent definition it was created from,
    a *brief* and one or more turns. A task id is sz- followed by six base32
    characters. The brief is the first self-contained text that the child
    receives, and the child sees nothing of the parent conversation except the
    brief and each follow-up message.

    A *follow-up message* is a self-contained text that the caller gives a task
    after its brief. A *turn* is one input to a task, the brief or a
    follow-up message, processed until its child run settles, and it has one
    *child run*. Each turn has a *turn number*, counted from 1 for the turn of
    the brief. A *steering message* is a follow-up message that is delivered
    into the turn that is running instead of opening a new turn.

    A *delivery outcome* says what became of one follow-up message: queued,
    when it is held for a turn whose child run has not reached Live, steered,
    when it was delivered as a steering message, new turn, when it opened a
    new turn, or not delivered, when no child received it.

    Each turn holds its *enqueue time*, the time the turn opened, and, once
    its caller has released its child run, its *start time*, the time of that
    release. The enqueue time of turn 1 is the time the task was created.

    A child run has a *child run state*. It is Pending until its caller
    releases it, then moves through Starting and Live to Settled, and it can
    also end Failed or Cancelled. A child run is Live from the moment its
    child driver reports that it sent its prompt.

    A failed child run carries one *failure kind*, whose value is timeout, crashed,
    error
    or skill_mismatch. A cancelled child run carries no failure kind.

    A result has a status of completed, truncated, failed or cancelled. It
    holds
    the task id first, a *summary* and the *session usage*, and a failed result
    also holds its failure kind. The result of a turn whose turn number is 2 or
    more also holds that turn number, and the result of a turn whose child run
    ended before it sent its prompt says in one line that its held
    follow-up messages were not delivered. The status truncated means only that the
    child
    stopped at its own length limit. The summary is the whole last assistant
    message of the child run.

    A session usage is the token count, the cost and the context use at settle
    of a
    child run, as its driver reports them. Each field is optional, and a field
    that
    the driver cannot report is unavailable and never zero.

    A *launch spec* is the record of how a driver started a child run, without
    its
    prompt: the command as one opaque list, the *child working directory*, the
    names of
    its
    environment variables and never their values, the version of the child
    program, the skill folder paths and the thinking level that the driver set
    for
    the child run. A field that the driver cannot report is unavailable.

    A *domain event* is one step of a child run, such as run started, skills
    verified, prompt sent, tool activity, agent message, follow-up message,
    turn settled and run exited. The brief event carries the whole brief, and
    the follow-up message event carries the whole text of the follow-up
    message it records. Tool
    activity carries the tool name and its target path, and the run started
    event
    carries the launch spec of the child run. Each domain event is written to
    the
    event log of its task.

    A *child session* is the record of the child's own conversation that its
    child driver keeps for a task, in the sessions folder that TaskStore
    provides. Every turn of a task adds to the same child session.

    A child driver may offer *turn steering*, which delivers a
    steering message into its live child run, and *session resume*, which
    starts a child run on the existing child session of a task with a
    follow-up message as its prompt. Both are optional.
  }

  logic {
    On a task request, create the task with a new task id, the frozen
    agent definition, the brief and the enqueue time, and write the task record
    with the status running, the model of the agent definition and the
    enqueue time. The task id goes to the result and to the child port, and the
    agent definition goes to the child port with the project root, the
    sessions folder, the folder of the driver files of the task and the child
    host options that the request carries. The
    task record
    goes to
    the final
    step, which writes the result into it. The brief opens turn 1, and it is
    written whole to the event log as the brief event. Keep the
    child run of each turn Pending until its caller releases it. On release, take
    the start time and write it into the task record. A turn whose prompt is
    the brief, turn 1 or the release of a task that never sent its prompt,
    goes on to the brief check, which confirms that the task holds exactly one
    brief and passes that brief to the child port, which starts the child run
    and ends this part of the flow. A turn opened by a follow-up message hands
    that follow-up message to the session resume of the child driver, which
    starts the child run on the child session and ends this part of the
    flow.

    While the child run is live, write each domain event to the event log and
    offer
    it to the caller, which ends that branch. When the run started event
    arrives,
    write the launch spec it carries into the task record without reading it,
    which
    ends that branch. When the prompt sent event arrives, the child run is
    Live, and hand each held follow-up message, in the order received, to the
    turn steering of the child driver, which ends that branch. When a
    child run that sent its prompt settles while follow-up messages are still
    held for it, or turn steering reports for a held follow-up message that
    the child run had already settled, open a new turn for those messages as
    for a settled turn, with the first as its prompt and the rest held for
    it, which ends that branch. When a child run fails or is cancelled before
    it sent its prompt, its held follow-up messages are not delivered and its
    result says so, so that a task that fails at each start never loops. When
    the child run settles, build the result with the
    task id
    first, the whole last assistant text as the summary and the session usage.
    The result
    goes to the final step. A failed child run builds its result with its
    failure kind, and a cancelled child run builds it with the status cancelled
    and
    no failure kind. Both go to the same final step. In the final step, write
    the
    result into the task record and return the result to the caller, which ends
    the flow.

    On a follow-up message for a task, write it whole to the event log as a
    follow-up message event, then act on the child run state of the latest
    turn. While that child run is Pending or Starting and the child driver
    offers turn steering, hold the follow-up message for that turn and return
    the delivery outcome queued, which ends the flow; when it offers no
    turn steering, return the delivery outcome not delivered, which ends the
    flow. While it is Live, hand the follow-up message to the turn steering
    of the child driver. When the child driver reports that it delivered it,
    which restarts the timeout of that child run in the child driver, return
    the delivery outcome steered, which ends the flow. When it reports that the
    child run had
    already settled, go on as for a settled turn. A latest turn that ended
    with no result, because the process that ran it died, counts as settled
    here. When the latest turn has
    settled and the task has a child session, open a new turn with the next
    turn number and a new enqueue time, whose child run starts Pending and is
    released by the caller
    like the first one, pass the follow-up message to the session resume of
    the child driver, and return the delivery outcome new turn, which ends the
    flow. When the latest turn has settled and the task has no child session,
    because no child run of it ever sent its prompt, open a new turn in the
    same way, start its child run with the brief as its prompt and hold the
    follow-up message for it, and return the delivery outcome new turn, which
    ends the flow. When the child driver offers neither turn steering nor
    session resume for the case at hand, return the delivery outcome
    not delivered, which ends the flow. Each new turn ends in the same final
    step, which writes its result into the task record and returns it.
  }

  constraints {
    A task has at most one live child run, and each turn produces exactly one
    result, so a task request with no follow-up message produces exactly one
    result. A steering message never produces a result of its own, and the
    result of its turn carries no count of steering messages.

    Every turn of a task runs on the frozen agent definition of the task, its
    model and its thinking level included, and a follow-up message never
    changes them.

    The timeout of a child run restarts each time a steering message is
    delivered into it, and each new turn gets the whole timeout of the
    agent definition. Nothing bounds the total time of a task across its
    steering messages and turns.

    A new turn that opens because a child run had already settled, for held
    follow-up messages or for a follow-up message, must open only after the
    final step of the previous turn has written its result into the
    task record, so that no two child runs of a task are live at once and
    the result of the earlier turn comes first in the task record.

    Held follow-up messages must be delivered in the order received and only
    after the child run has sent its prompt, so that the brief, or the
    follow-up message that opened the turn, is always the first input of the
    turn.

    TaskCore must reach a child only through the *child port* and must not
    import
    types specific to one child host. TaskCore requires the child port of the
    child host of the
    agent definition, which a child driver provides and its caller hands over,
    and
    does not depend on any particular child driver.

    TaskCore requires the task record and depends on TaskStore, which provides
    it. Only TaskCore may ask TaskStore to write a task record, and TaskStore
    performs the write.

    The result must not carry the *transcript*, *tool output* or *skill content* of
    the
    child run.

    TaskCore must not record or compute which files a child run changed, must
    not
    snapshot or copy *workspace files* and must not gate writes. The child run is
    limited only by its tool list, and what it writes is for the user to review
    with git, including when a parent model and a child write the same files.

    A cancelled child run that is still Pending must never start a child. It
    has the status cancelled, no failure kind, no session usage and no summary.
    A cancel ends only the turn that is running, and the task can still take a
    follow-up message afterwards.

    The summary must never be cut, whatever its length or status, and an empty
    summary stays empty. TaskCore holds no size for a summary: a front end that
    knows how its parent host receives a result may tell the child a size in
    the
    brief.

    A field of the session usage that the child run cannot report must be
    marked
    unavailable and never reported as zero.

    A launch spec must never hold the value of an environment variable, because
    the task record is plain text, and TaskCore must write the launch spec into
    the
    task record unread and must not depend on any flag that it holds.

    The start time must be taken when the caller releases the child run and
    never read from the launch spec, and a child run that is still Pending
    has no start time.

    TaskCore offers no ask, fork or rerun of a task.
  }

  cases {
    A summary of 60,000 characters, or of a truncated result, is returned whole
    with its status unchanged, and the task id is still first in the result.

    A child run whose last assistant message has the stop reason error is
    Failed
    with the failure kind error, and a cancelled child run carries no
    failure kind.

    The run started event of a child run puts its launch spec in the
    task record
    before the child run settles.

    A task whose child run stays Pending longer than its timeout still runs
    once
    it is released, and its start time is later than its enqueue time.

    A follow-up message for a task whose child run is Pending returns queued,
    and once its child driver reports the prompt sent event it is delivered by
    turn steering. A follow-up message for a live child run returns steered,
    the timeout of that child run starts again, and the turn settles with one
    result. A follow-up message for a settled turn returns new turn, and the
    result of that turn holds turn number 2 and comes from the same
    child session.

    A follow-up message for a task whose only child run was cancelled while
    Pending returns new turn, and its child run sends the brief first and then
    the follow-up message as a steering message.

    A follow-up message that reaches a child driver after its child run settled
    opens a new turn instead, and the caller gets new turn and not steered.

    A follow-up message held for a task whose child run then fails with the
    failure kind skill_mismatch before its prompt is not delivered, the result
    of that turn says so, and no new turn opens. A follow-up message held for a
    child run that settles before its child driver delivers it opens a new turn
    with it as the prompt.
  }

  interface {
    Accept a task request made of an agent definition, a brief, the child port
    of
    the child host of the agent definition, the child host options for that
    child host and an optional parent session id.
    Return the
    result, which never carries the transcript, tool output or skill content, with
    the task id first once the child run settles, or let the caller leave the
    task
    running and ask for its result later. A child run stays in the
    child run state Pending until its caller releases it, so that the caller
    can
    limit how many child runs run at the same time.

    Offer the child run state, the enqueue time and the start time of a live
    task to the caller, so that it can tell a Pending child run from a released
    one.

    Accept a follow-up message for a task, also for a task that is not live
    in this process, which TaskCore first loads from its task record with its
    frozen agent definition, its brief, its turns and its child session, and
    return its delivery outcome:
    hold it while the child run of the latest turn is Pending or Starting,
    deliver it as a steering message while that child run is Live, and open a
    new turn when that turn has settled, returning the result of the new turn
    with its turn number once its child run settles.

    Cancel a live child run as cancelled, and return the result.

    Offer each domain event of a live child run to the caller as it happens,
    and
    offer a failure kind in the result of a failed child run.

    Define the child port that a *child driver* implements: start a child run
    for a
    task id, an agent definition, a brief, the project root, the
    sessions folder,
    the folder of the driver files of the task and the child host options for
    its child host, so that a child driver
    resolves
    none of these itself and reads no task record. Hand its launch spec, including
    its child working directory, to the
    caller in the run started domain event, run it to settlement, cancel it,
    and
    report the prompt sent domain event once it has sent its prompt, and
    report the outcome, which is the last assistant text, the status, the
    failure kind of a failed run and the session usage.

    A child driver may also offer turn steering, which delivers a
    steering message into its live child run and reports that it delivered it
    or that the child run had already settled, and session resume, which starts
    a child run on the existing child session of a task with a follow-up message
    as its prompt and is otherwise started, run, cancelled and reported like
    any child run. A child driver may also offer an *attach command* that
    reopens the
    child session of a task in the terminal interface of its child program,
    built
    from the launch spec of the task, a *session export* that writes the
    child session of a task as a document that a person can read, and its
    *starter tools*, which are the tool list that suits a general agent on its
    child host
    and the tool list that only reads and suits a fallback agent on its
    child host,
    and
    a *prerequisites check*, which passes or returns a message that says what
    its
    child host lacks and what to do, a *model listing*, which takes the
    child host options for its child host and
    gives each model that a child run on its child host can use with the thinking
    levels that
    model accepts, its model endpoint, its context size, its cost and whether
    it reasons, or a one-line reason why it cannot, and a *model check*, which
    takes the same child host options and
    sends one fixed prompt to one model at one thinking level as a child run
    would and reports that the model answered, or that it did not with the
    kind unknown_model, timeout, failed or cancelled and a one-line reason. A
    caller must ask for any of the eight through the child driver of the
    child host it works with, such as that of a task, an agent or the
    guided setup, and must not assume
    that a child driver offers it.
  }

  decisions {
    A follow-up message replaces the one brief of the spike, because a child
    that goes the wrong way could only be cancelled and started again, losing
    its work. One way in, chosen by the state of the task, spares the caller a
    race with a child that settles just before its message arrives. A
    steering message stays in its turn so that a steered turn still settles
    once, and a follow-up message for a task that never sent its prompt
    starts it with its brief, so that the caller need not write the brief
    again. Cancelled
    is
    a status and not a failure kind, because a failure kind belongs to a failed
    result that a parent model received. The launch spec is a shape of TaskCore
    and
    not
    of any child driver, so that TaskCore, TaskStore and Cli carry no driver
    type.
    The summary is never cut, because a cut answer or cut JSON fails the
    parent model
    in a way that depends only on how long the child wrote, and the size a
    parent model
    can take inline belongs to the front end that returns it. TaskCore keeps no
    list of changed files because git is the record and the review of
    concurrent
    writers is the user's.
  }
}
